@[expose] public section

universe u

open CategoryTheory
open AlgebraicGeometry

namespace AlgebraicGeometry

/-- Weak relative Noether normalization.

Source: Stacks Project, More on Morphisms, Tag 0GTG, label
`lemma-weak-relative-noether-normalization`.

(blueprint: lem-trf-weak-relative-noether-normalization)

Proof (the nine steps of the node, following Tag 0GTG):

1. Use [lem-weil-pbc-lft-affine-pairs] on the given affine morphism to see that $A$ is a finite
type $R$-algebra. We will prove the stronger conclusion with exactly $N$ coordinates.
Throughout, if $C$ is generated by finitely many elements over $B$, then
$\operatorname{Spec}(C)\to\operatorname{Spec}(B)$ is of finite type: the global affine pair
gives local finite type by [lem-weil-pbc-lft-pointwise-criterion], and the morphism is affine by
[lem-weil-pbc-morphism-affine-schemes-affine], hence quasi-compact by
[lem-weil-pbc-affine-morphism-permanence]. If $B$ is enlarged to a polynomial ring mapping to
$C$, the same generators still generate $C$. The affine fibre-product formula
[lem-weil-pbc-fibre-product-affine-spectra] identifies fibres with spectra of the tensor
products with residue fields. These observations apply to every ring map constructed below.

2. First reduce to finite presentation. Write $A=R[x_1,\ldots,x_m]/J$ using
[def-weil-pbc-finite-type-algebra]. Index the finitely generated subideals $J_\lambda\subseteq
J$ by inclusion; this is a small nonempty directed poset because sums give upper bounds. Put
$A_\lambda=R[x_1,\ldots,x_m]/J_\lambda$. These algebras are finitely presented
[def-weil-pbc-finitely-presented-algebra], their transition maps are surjective, and their
colimit is $A$: every polynomial relation in $J$ belongs to a finitely generated subideal. Apply
[lem-weil-pbc-affine-limit-fibre-dimension-bound] with constant base $R$ and bound $N$ to obtain
a stage whose fibres all have dimension at most $N$. A quasi-finite map $R[T_1,\ldots,T_N]\to
A_\lambda$, if obtained, gives one to $A$ by composition with the quotient: the same algebra
generators descend, and for each target prime the primes of the quotient fibre identify with the
subset of primes of the old fibre containing the quotient ideal. The old fibres are affine and
quasi-compact by [lem-weil-pbc-affine-morphism-permanence], and quasi-finiteness makes all their
points isolated; the singleton open cover therefore has a finite subcover. Thus each new fibre
is a subspace of a finite discrete fibre, hence finite and discrete. This is quasi-finiteness
[pqf-def-quasifinite]. It suffices to prove the claim for finitely presented algebras.

3. For finitely presented $R\to A$, apply [lem-weil-pbc-limit-module-finite-presentation] with
the zero module (which is finitely presented). After a common universe enlargement, its indexing
set is small. It gives a directed system $R_\lambda\to A_\lambda$ with colimit $R\to A$, each
$R_\lambda$ finite type over $\mathbf Z$, each $A_\lambda$ finite type over $R_\lambda$, and
comparison and limit base-change isomorphisms. In particular $A_\lambda$ is finite type over
$\mathbf Z$: adjoin its finitely many algebra generators to the finitely many ring generators of
$R_\lambda$. The comparison maps are isomorphisms, hence surjective, so
[lem-weil-pbc-affine-limit-fibre-dimension-bound] gives a stage with all fibre dimensions at
most $N$. A quasi-finite map $R_\lambda[T_1,\ldots,T_N]\to A_\lambda$ is locally quasi-finite by
the forward direction of [pqf-lem-qf-locqf-qc]. It base changes to the required map over $R$:
finite type is preserved by [lem-sfb-finite-type-composition-base-change] and local
quasi-finiteness by clause (2) of [lem-weil-pbc-separated-lqf-base-change]. A finite type
morphism is quasi-compact, so the converse direction of [pqf-lem-qf-locqf-qc] shows the result
is quasi-finite [pqf-def-quasifinite]. The target tensor product is $R[T_1,\ldots,T_N]$: the
isomorphism sends $p\otimes r$ to $r p$ with coefficients mapped to $R$; its inverse sends $r$
to $1\otimes r$ and each variable to $T_i\otimes1$. The source tensor product is $A$ by the
cited limit theorem and the affine fibre-product formula. We are reduced to $R,A$ finite type
over $\mathbf Z$.

4. For rings $R,A$ finite type over $\mathbf Z$, induct on the bound $N$, simultaneously for all
such ring maps. When $N=0$, each fibre is a finite-type scheme over its residue field by
[lem-sfb-finite-type-composition-base-change]. It is finite and discrete by
[lem-weil-pbc-finite-type-dim-zero-artinian]. The original finite-type morphism is therefore
quasi-finite [pqf-def-quasifinite], giving the zero-variable map $R\to A$. Empty fibres cause no
exception. For $N>0$, assume the result for $N-1$ over every base of finite type over $\mathbf
Z$. We will construct $c\in A$ such that all fibres of $R[t]\to A$, $t\mapsto c$, have dimension
at most $N-1$, before applying that outer induction hypothesis.

5. For this coordinate construction with fixed $N>0$, use a second induction on $\dim R$
[def-weil-pbc-krull-dimension-ring]. This is a finite integer when $R\ne0$: write $R$ as a
quotient of $\mathbf Z[x_1,\ldots,x_m]$ [def-weil-pbc-finite-type-algebra]. Every ideal of
$\mathbf Z$ is principal by the division algorithm (choose its least positive member if
nonzero); hence $\mathbf Z$ is Noetherian, since the union of an ascending chain of ideals is
generated by one element already in a stage. Its dimension is at most one by
[lem-trf-principal-ideal-ring-dimension-bound], and the polynomial dimension formula
[lem-sf-noetherian-polynomial-dimension] bounds the polynomial ring dimension by $m+1$. Inverse
images lift any strict chain of primes of a quotient to a strict chain in that polynomial ring,
so $\dim R\le m+1$. Treat $R=0$ separately: then its unital algebra $A$ is zero and $c=0$ works.
If $A=0$ the same choice works over any $R$. The inner induction hypothesis is the coordinate
construction for every finite-type $\mathbf Z$-base of strictly smaller dimension, with the same
bound $N$.

6. For nonzero $R$ and $A$, apply [lem-trf-generic-principal-coordinate-dimension-drop] with
$S=A$ and $d=N$. Obtain $a_0\in A$ and $f\in R$ avoiding every minimal prime of $R$, such that
$R_f[t]\to A_f$, $t\mapsto a_0$, has all fibres of dimension at most $N-1$. Replace $a_0$ by
$a=f a_0\in fA$. This keeps the bound over $R_f$: the new map is the old map precomposed with
the automorphism of $R_f[t]$ sending $t$ to $f t$, whose inverse sends $t$ to $f^{-1}t$. This
only relabels the target points and identifies the corresponding fibres. Inverting $f$ has the
meaning of [def-weil-ring-localization-away].

7. The quotient $R/fR$ has strictly smaller dimension than $R$, unless it is zero (the
empty-base case already handled). Indeed lift a prime chain of $R/fR$ to a chain $\mathfrak
p_0\subsetneq\cdots\subsetneq\mathfrak p_s$ in $R$, every member containing $f$. By
[lem-weil-pbc-prime-contains-minimal-prime], choose a minimal prime $\mathfrak
q\subseteq\mathfrak p_0$. Since $f\notin\mathfrak q$, this inclusion is strict; adjoining
$\mathfrak q$ gives a chain one longer, so $s+1\le\dim R$ [def-weil-pbc-krull-dimension-ring].
Both $R/fR$ and $A/fA$ remain finite type over $\mathbf Z$, by taking images of generators.
Their fibres are the original fibres over primes containing $f$: for such a prime $\mathfrak p$,
$(A/fA)\otimes_{R/fR}\kappa(\mathfrak p/fR)\cong A\otimes_R\kappa(\mathfrak p)$, via the maps
induced by $\bar a\otimes u\mapsto a\otimes u$ and its inverse. Thus their fibre dimensions are
still at most $N$. The inner induction gives $\bar b\in A/fA$ for which $(R/fR)[t]\to A/fA$,
$t\mapsto\bar b$, has all fibre dimensions at most $N-1$. If the quotient is zero take $\bar
b=0$. Choose a lift $b\in A$.

8. Apply [lem-trf-power-perturbation-fibre-dimension] to the finite type map $R_f\to A_f$, the
elements $a,b$, and the nonnegative bound $N-1$. Choose $n\ge\max(1,n_0)$ and set $c=a^n+b\in
A$. Over $R_f$ the map $t\mapsto c$ has fibre dimension at most $N-1$. Modulo $f$, $a\in fA$ and
$n>0$ imply $\bar c=\bar b$, so the same bound holds on the quotient. To check every fibre of
$R[t]\to A$, let $Q\subset R[t]$ be prime and put $\mathfrak p=Q\cap R$. If $f\notin\mathfrak
p$, localizing at $f$ does not change this fibre: $A_f\otimes_{R_f[t]}\kappa(QR_f[t])\cong
A\otimes_{R[t]}\kappa(Q)$, since $f$ is invertible in this field. If $f\in\mathfrak p$, the
fibre is $(A/fA)\otimes_{(R/fR)[t]}\kappa(Q/fR[t])$, because $f$ acts by zero. The residue
fields here are the original $\kappa(Q)$, being fraction fields of the same quotient domain, and
the tensor identifications send the image of $x\otimes u$ to $x\otimes u$
[lem-weil-pbc-fibre-product-affine-spectra]. Every prime is in exactly one case, proving the
coordinate construction and closing the inner induction.

9. The ring $R[t]$ is finite type over $\mathbf Z$ (adjoin $t$ to generators of $R$), and $A$ is
finite type over $R[t]$ with $t\mapsto c$ (retain its generators over $R$)
[def-weil-pbc-finite-type-algebra]. Apply the outer induction hypothesis with bound $N-1$ to
this new map. It gives a quasi-finite map $(R[t])[u_1,\ldots,u_{N-1}]\to A$. Identify its source
with $R[T_1,\ldots,T_N]$ by $T_1=t$ and $T_{i+1}=u_i$; the inverse is the same substitution in
reverse. This is the desired map with exactly $N$ coordinates, closing the outer induction.
Steps 2 and 3 then give that map for the original $R$ and $A$. Take $d=N$, so $0\le d\le N$.
-/
theorem weakRelativeNoetherNormalization
    {R A : Type u} [CommRing R] [CommRing A] [Algebra R A]
    (hft :
      LocallyOfFiniteType
          (Scheme.Spec.map (Opposite.op (CommRingCat.ofHom (algebraMap R A)))) ∧
        QuasiCompact
          (Scheme.Spec.map (Opposite.op (CommRingCat.ofHom (algebraMap R A)))))
    (N : ℕ)
    (hN : ∀ y,
      topologicalKrullDim
          ((Scheme.Spec.map (Opposite.op (CommRingCat.ofHom (algebraMap R A)))).fiber y) ≤
        (N : ℕ∞)) :
    ∃ d : ℕ, 0 ≤ d ∧ d ≤ N ∧
      ∃ g : MvPolynomial (Fin d) R →ₐ[R] A,
        Scheme.Hom.IsQuasiFinite
          (Scheme.Spec.map (Opposite.op (CommRingCat.ofHom g.toRingHom))) := by
  sorry

end AlgebraicGeometry
